19:["$","main",null,{"className":"docs_main__7o2m6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"docs_articleMeta__8zYHO","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"LESSON 02 OF 5"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Foundation"}]]}],["$","article",null,{"className":"docs_article__Ja_Vh","children":[["$","$1","0",{"children":[[["$","h1","h1-0",{"children":"Data, labels, and trustworthy splits"}],"\n",["$","p","p-0",{"children":"A model can look impressive in a test and fail in production because the test did not represent future use. This lesson treats the dataset as a product contract: what was known, how the answer was recorded, and how unseen examples will differ."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-0",{"id":"define-one-example-precisely","children":"Define one example precisely"}],"\n",["$","p","p-1",{"children":["For ticket routing, one ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"example"}]," is the ticket as it looked when it arrived. Its ",["$","strong","strong-1",{"children":"features"}]," may be the subject, body, language, and product area. Its ",["$","strong","strong-2",{"children":"label"}]," is the final queue chosen after review. A field such as “team that solved it” is not an input feature if it is only created after routing. A feature is useful only when it exists, with the same meaning, at inference time."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-2",{"children":"Labels can be noisy. A ticket may be transferred among teams, or an agent may select a convenient category rather than the best one. Write a label policy: use the final resolving queue, the first accepted queue, or a reviewed category? These choices define different learning problems. Sample and audit labels before choosing a model."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-1",{"id":"split-for-the-future-you-expect","children":"Split for the future you expect"}],"\n",["$","p","p-3",{"children":["Use a ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"training set"}]," to fit parameters, a ",["$","strong","strong-1",{"children":"validation set"}]," to compare versions or thresholds, and a ",["$","strong","strong-2",{"children":"test set"}]," for a final check after choices are made. Repeatedly tuning against the test set turns it into another validation set. Keep it separate until the candidate and decision rule are fixed."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-4",{"children":"A random row split is not always appropriate. If many tickets from the same customer or incident have near-identical text, copies in train and test make the test easier than future unseen customers. If product vocabulary changes over time, a later time window is more realistic than a random sample from the same period. The split unit should match the expected generalization claim."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-2",{"id":"diagram-preparing-evidence-for-evaluation","children":"Diagram: preparing evidence for evaluation"}],"\n",["$","pre","pre-0",{"children":["$","code","code-0",{"className":"language-aosdf-diagram","children":"Raw tickets ──▶ deduplicate / audit labels ──▶ group or time split\n                                                  ├──▶ training: fit\n                                                  ├──▶ validation: choose\n                                                  └──▶ test: final estimate\n"}]}],"\n",["$","p","p-5",{"children":["The diagram separates ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"data cleaning"}]," from ",["$","strong","strong-1",{"children":"model selection"}],". A clean split is not a substitute for representative data; the test distribution should still resemble the people and requests the feature will serve."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-6",{"children":[["$","$L13","a-0",{"href":"/ml-foundations-lifecycle.archify.html","children":"Explore the full ML lifecycle diagram"}]," to follow the data from the decision through the held-out evaluation."]}],"\n","$L25","\n","$L26","\n","$L27","\n","$L28","\n","$L29","\n","$L2a","\n","$L2b","\n","$L2c","\n","$L2d","\n","$L2e","\n","$L2f","\n","$L30","\n","$L31"],false]}]]}],"$L32"]}]
